{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# DC renderer robustness & performance hardening\n\n## Context / Problem\nThe DC path shells out to pandoc per field. Three hazards proven in the DC\nexperiment: (1) per-field subprocess tax — a flat ~0.3s/field spawn cost regardless of\nbody size; a serial reconcile blocked ~20 min of wall time on `fork+exec`; (2) pathological-input hang — one corpus body drove pandoc's jira reader\nto spin 13.5 min at 95.8% CPU, and pypandoc invokes pandoc with no timeout, so one\nfield can stall the reconcile indefinitely; (3) pandoc is GPL and rides in the\n`pypandoc-binary` wheel, so its provenance and license must be pinned and recorded.\n\n## Prior art / dependency boundaries (do NOT re-decide 271c's choices)\n- **The renderer module and its pandoc delivery are owned by 271c.** Story\n  `[rebar:271c-e88f-3220-4c9b]` (`repressive-delectable-swan`) has LANDED the pure\n  renderer at\n  `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_family/wiki_render.py`, and\n  pandoc is delivered by the pinned **`pypandoc-binary==1.15` wheel** via a `wiki`\n  optional extra (binary BUNDLED IN THE WHEEL, pinned in `pyproject.toml`), so the DC\n  path is self-contained in CI and prod with no system pandoc install. This story\n  hardens THAT module and pins THAT wheel; it does **not** add a system-level pandoc\n  or an `acli`-style version+sha binary pin.\n- 271c leaves `WikiTextCodec` fully identity and `wiki_render.py` a **pure module not\n  referenced by any codec op or live send path**; all live wiring (codec ops AND live\n  send sites) is deferred to the cutover story `[rebar:3388-96e8-e14e-4221]` and is\n  **out of scope here**. This story touches only `wiki_render.py` functions.\n- **The change-gated \"no-op → no pandoc spawn\" property is delivered by that cutover/\n  echo story**, which introduces the change-gated `rich_sha` render gate; that symbol\n  does not yet exist on `origin/main` and is not this story's to add. THIS story owns\n  only the cost, timeout, fallback, batching, and compliance of a render that IS\n  performed.\n\n## Approach\n- **Batch contiguous SAFE units to bound per-render subprocess cost:** 271c's landed\n  `_lock_and_split` emits units tagged render / table / exact / blank. This story groups\n  each **maximal run of contiguous SAFE (render) units** and feeds it to pandoc as **one\n  Markdown document → one wiki segment**, so many safe blocks cost one spawn per run,\n  not one per unit. **A run ABSORBS interleaved BLANK units**, re-emitting their exact\n  text as the blank lines between blocks inside that one document; a run breaks ONLY at\n  a table/exact (dangerous) unit. This is load-bearing: the landed splitter emits a\n  blank unit between every pair of blocks (measured: `render, blank, render, blank,\n  render, blank, table, …`), so if blanks broke a run then no run of length >1 could\n  exist and the batching would be a silent no-op. **No per-unit re-splitting of a\n  batch's output**: the combined wiki is one contiguous segment; reassembly is at the\n  segment (run) level. (pandoc 3.9 exposes no `--server`/daemon, so a persistent server\n  is out of scope.)\n- **Dangerous units are spliced VERBATIM — pandoc is never invoked on them.** Per 271c\n  a dangerous unit corrupts pandoc's jira reader, so it passes through as its original\n  Markdown at its original position between rendered segments. Order is restored by\n  concatenating the ordered segments — no delimiter/sentinel splitting, since each\n  invocation renders one contiguous safe run.\n- **Hard timeout + process-group reaping + batch-level verbatim fallback:** a plain\n  `subprocess.run(timeout=)` / pypandoc timeout reaps only the DIRECT child, so a\n  pandoc jira-reader spinning at ~95.8% CPU (a pipe-holding grandchild) survives\n  (repo bug d843, CPython bpo-30154). So the renderer MUST NOT use pypandoc's\n  high-level `convert_text`/`convert_file` API (no process handle); it locates pandoc\n  via `pypandoc.get_pandoc_path()` and builds its OWN\n  `subprocess.Popen([pandoc_path, …], start_new_session=True)`, **enforces the\n  wall-clock timeout caller-side via `proc.communicate(timeout=dc_pandoc_timeout_s)`\n  and, on `subprocess.TimeoutExpired`, passes that `Popen`** to the leaf\n  **`rebar._proc.reap_process_group`** (`_proc.py:26`) —\n  `killpg` SIGTERM→grace→SIGKILL→bounded drain, already reused by the ACLI transport\n  and grounding harness — killing the whole group, not just the direct child. It\n  kills/drains only AFTER a timeout is detected, so caller-side detection is required\n  (the `acli_subprocess.py` pattern). **The timeout is a config key**\n  (`reconciler.dc_pandoc_timeout_s`, default **10s**) from the DC experiment: >30× the\n  observed ~0.3s/field normal render and ~80× below the 13.5-min pathological hang;\n  `grace`/`drain` reuse the ACLI caller's existing constants. On a\n  safe-run call's timeout or non-zero exit, degrade **that entire safe-run segment**\n  to its original Markdown verbatim (trivially echo-safe) and record a **segment-level\n  fallback marker**; other segments and the verbatim dangerous units are unaffected.\n  The fallback boundary is exactly the pandoc-invocation boundary.\n- **Compliance:** pin the wheel in `pyproject.toml`, record pandoc's GPL license in\n  the SBOM/notice, verify the pin in CI.\n\n## Scope\n- `adapters/jira_family/wiki_render.py` — own\n  `subprocess.Popen(start_new_session=True)` over `pypandoc.get_pandoc_path()`,\n  per-invocation timeout + `rebar._proc.reap_process_group`, contiguous-safe-run\n  batching, verbatim dangerous units, segment-level fallback (all as above).\n- `pyproject.toml` — pin the `pypandoc-binary` wheel version (extends 271c's pin).\n- config (`reconciler.dc_pandoc_timeout_s`, default 10s) — the per-invocation\n  timeout, tunable without a code change. A `reconciler.*` key has a fixed multi-file\n  surface here (mirrors `reconciler.jira_cli_timeout`); all are updated together:\n  `src/rebar/_config_schema.py` — add field `dc_pandoc_timeout_s: float = 10.0` to\n  `ReconcilerConfig` plus validator entry `_as_float(v, k, minimum=0.0)` alongside\n  `jira_cli_timeout`; `src/rebar/_config_sources.py` — a `_CANONICAL_ENV_NAMES` alias\n  only if a non-derived env name is wanted (otherwise the auto-derived\n  `REBAR_RECONCILER_DC_PANDOC_TIMEOUT_S` applies);\n  `tests/golden/config_surface.json` — deliberately REGENERATE to add the entry (the\n  guard `tests/unit/test_config_surface_golden.py` is removal-only, so it will NOT flag\n  a new live key's omission); `docs/config.md` — document it in the\n  `[tool.rebar.reconciler]` block. A unit test mirrors the existing\n  `test_config_file_wiring.py` four-consumer pattern.\n- CI workflow config — verify the `pypandoc-binary` wheel pin (NOT an acli-style sha\n  check).\n- SBOM/licensing notice file — record the wheel-bundled pandoc GPL entry.\n- `tests/` — timeout, fallback, and batching tests over the vendored DC corpus.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every pandoc invocation runs under a caller-side wall-clock timeout via\n  `proc.communicate(timeout=dc_pandoc_timeout_s)` (config\n  `reconciler.dc_pandoc_timeout_s`, default 10s — above the ~0.3s normal render, below\n  the 13.5-min hang) and, on `subprocess.TimeoutExpired`, process-group reaping via\n  `rebar._proc.reap_process_group` (the CPU-spinning grandchild is killed,\n  not orphaned); on timeout or non-zero exit it degrades that safe-run segment\n  to verbatim Markdown without raising and the render returns; verified by\n  `test_dc_pandoc_timeout_falls_back` (asserts the group is reaped) and\n  `test_dc_pandoc_error_degrades`.\n- [ ] A changed body of N contiguous safe units renders in one pandoc invocation (one\n  wiki segment) with original block order preserved, dangerous units are spliced\n  VERBATIM with no pandoc call, and a safe-run timeout degrades exactly that run's\n  segment (segment boundary == fallback boundary); verified by\n  `test_dc_render_batches_safe_run`, `test_dc_dangerous_unit_verbatim_no_pandoc`, and\n  `test_dc_segment_fallback_boundary`.\n- [ ] The pandoc binary comes from the `pypandoc-binary` wheel pinned by version in\n  `pyproject.toml` (271c's mechanism, not a system pandoc), the pin is verified in CI,\n  and its GPL license is recorded in the SBOM/notice; verified by the CI wheel-pin\n  check and a notice-file test."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786593397354201001,"uuid":"6af1b71e-e5be-4041-8d04-c32ef0b416ec"}